eries i am bringing with me all of my  knowledge of how these games  work and take advantage of that nailing  headshots avoiding enemies you don't  need a kill  scavenging every possible nook and  cranny for items and money  this game absolutely rewards this kind  of play  on standard you'll often find yourself  feeling a bit too  powerful and prepared than what the  setting might suggest  this of course has its own benefits not  worrying as much about ammo for easy to  focus more on the act of combat itself  which gets really fun when the hordes of  lichen start to flood in  so before i go any further i should say  that it is  one thousand percent okay to play the  game on standard if that's what you  enjoy  i personally like that terrifying  feeling of having your back against the  wall  that's why i love the series but some  people just want something a little more  relaxed and that's okay  you also might be the kind of player  whose skill level is more attuned for  standard  and will find it to be the right level  of challenge so don't let anyone tell  you you're lesser for it  difficulties exist for a reason and  people play games differently  all of that said if you crave that  resource management  the stress of not having enough first  aid and love it when one wrong move can  turn a good run  into sheer tension then i'm gonna  recommend you play your first time on  hardcore  after finishing the game i loaded up a  fresh save on hardcore to see if it  scratched that  itch and i was not disappointed  it only takes a swing or two to go from  full health to into the red  leaving a lot less room for air when it  comes to getting hit  playing it safe learning the right time  to dodge and running a hallway  back to give yourself distance becomes  mandatory  even with weaker foes it's not that it's  hard  is that it asks you to put your all into  each and every fight  and always be alert something that i  never quite felt on standard  since damage from enemy hits were more  lacks  i also found myself engaging with the  block more often  something i rarely touched on standard  because i  never felt in enough danger that i  needed to rely on it  it's in hardcore that you realize how  important blocking really is  the damage reduced when blocking is a  lot more significant than you might  think  probably because you wouldn't think  blocking an axe swing with your hands  would be  all that effective but seriously the  difference between taking a hit and  blocking  is huge you're so vulnerable and  hardcore that you need to use it  all the time particularly in close  encounter fights where it becomes  basically mandatory blocking also  opens up a counter move where you can  push enemies away  and sometimes even disarm them of their  weapon a key strategy you'll want to  take advantage of  and another thing that i never touched  on standard  finally there is ammo and crafting the  amount of ammo and crafting materials  gained remains the same as standard  but with enemies taking more bullets to  dispatch the balance feels much more  aligned with what i was looking for  you always have just barely enough ammo  to make it through an encounter  sometimes with only a few bullets  remaining and the sudden stress of  needing to find more before the next  thing jumps at you from around a corner  yet it doesn't swing in the other  direction of being unfair  there was only one moment where i  completely ran out of ammo  and it's because i was being careless  and wasting them on enemies in an  optional area i was backtracking to and  probably should have just dodged around  them  a decision i never even contemplated on  standard but  often considered wall on hardcore on  hardcore you truly appreciate how well  done  item placement and resource balance is  and when it's not  bullets it's crafting materials which  you guessed it  i actually needed to use this time  having to make the tough choice to craft  a few more shotgun shells but lose  material needed for a first  aid is something that added a strategic  layer to the hardcore experience  despite only taking a break for a few  days between playthroughs  hardcore felt very fresh the game  demanded that i engage with all of its  systems and played smart  it took what i already thought was a  pretty fantastic survival horror  experience  and elevated it to one of the best  survival horror experiences i've ever  had  okay have i convinced you that hardcore  is pretty cool awesome  well i'm thrilled but i should warn you  about one thing  in a loving homage to resident evil 4  the first major combat encounter is a  harrowing survival where you are swamped  by  way too many enemies to shock your  system before things  slow back down and settle into a more  natural rhythm  it's a great set piece on standard but  it's kind of frustrating on hardcore  despite already beating the game i died  multiple times during this moment  the coolness of it slowly washing away  leaving only frustration  as i kept getting hit in the back by  that one guy jesus  stop doing that  it's a rough sequence especially if  you're still learning how the game is  played  maybe you'll be fine with it but i  wanted to bring it up as it was the  one sour note in my hardcore experience  the good thing is that once the moment  has  passed the game's pacing and combat  settles  and it's just excellent from then on so  if you can get past the opening  encounter  you're golden so there you go if  everything i just talked about  sounds like something you're interested  in hardcore might be for you  and i would really recommend taking a  risk and giving it a shot however i do  want to reiterate but i don't think  standard mode is the wrong way to  experience this game  it's more that it won't demand full use  of village's gameplay systems  and that in itself comes with the  benefit of having a smoother playthrough  meanwhile hardcore demands you learn and  master the game  weigh the cost and be